WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:09.720
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:13.560
So in this lecture, we
talk about nuclear fusion.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.320
And what we mean by fusion
is the energy production

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.300
by two light nuclei
fusing together

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:21.900
to produce a heavier one
which is more tightly bound.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:26.850
And again, we can understand
this from the empirical mass

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:28.680
formula, OK?

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:30.720
So the difficulty
in nuclear fusion

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.020
is that we now have to
overcome the Coulomb

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:35.480
barrier from the other side.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:38.700
So we have to bring two
light nuclei together, have

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:45.620
to overcome the Coulomb barrier
in order to form this heavier

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:49.600
and more tightly bound state.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:51.370
You might think
that you can just

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:55.000
take a two beams of protons
like we do at the LHC,

00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:00.110
bombard them, and
create heavier nuclei.

00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:02.440
But the problem is
that most of the nuclei

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:04.840
will scatter
elastically, and will not

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:08.510
lead to a new-bound state.

00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:11.300
So the practical way to
overcome the Coulomb barrier

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:19.700
is by creating a confined
mixture and supplying heat,

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:25.010
such that the thermal energy is
enough to overcome the Coulomb

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:26.410
barrier.

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:30.260
You can estimate how
much energy is needed.

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:35.540
If you, for example, assume a
Coulomb barrier of about 5 MeV,

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:40.370
this implies temperatures of
5 times 10 to the 10 Kelvin.

00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:43.760
OK, so that's really,
really hot, right?

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:48.800
If you compare this to typical
temperatures within stars,

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:53.710
you'll find that those are
only 10 to the 8 Kelvin.

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:56.420
So now you ask, why
does it still work?

00:01:56.420 --> 00:02:01.400
Why do we see fusion
within a stellar medium?

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:04.760
And the answer to this is,
again, quantum tunneling,

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:07.070
and to some degree,
it's also the fact

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:11.000
that when we have a medium
of a specific temperature,

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:15.850
the kinetic energy of
the particle involved

00:02:15.850 --> 00:02:18.810
follows a mixture of
energy distribution.

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:22.130
So you find some particles
which have enough energy

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:24.680
to overcome the
Coulomb potential.

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:29.720
Even so, the mean
value would be below.

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:33.320
The processes
within the sun, they

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:36.980
are dominated by so-called
proton-proton cycle, or PPI

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:37.970
cycle.

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:40.150
And this happens in
a number of steps.

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:43.370
You start with
hydrogen or protons--

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:46.190
the core of the sun
is basically a plasma.

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:49.525
So we can forget about
electrons in this context.

00:02:49.525 --> 00:02:53.620
So we have two protons
fusing together

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:57.220
to a deuteron, which is
a proton and a neutron,

00:02:57.220 --> 00:02:59.020
via the weak interaction.

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:03.880
Here, you find for the first
time, again, the neutrinos

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:06.150
being produced in the sun.

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:10.180
Then, you supply
the deuterium again.

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:12.340
And together with
the proton, you

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:14.440
are able to produce the helium.

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:16.480
And then the helium,
again, is being

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:19.240
used to supply the
third step in this.

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:22.495
Helium-3 is supplied
to produce helium-4.

00:03:22.495 --> 00:03:28.130
So this then-- the end product
is a helium-4 here, and energy.

00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:30.520
You combine all of
those three steps,

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:33.550
you find that you start
with four protons.

00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:39.070
You produce helium-4 plus
positrons, neutrinos, photons,

00:03:39.070 --> 00:03:41.450
and energy.

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:46.150
In fact, as I was
just saying, this all

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:49.630
happens within the hot plasma.

00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:51.820
The positrons are
basically annihilated

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:55.270
with electrons, which
are part of the plasma,

00:03:55.270 --> 00:04:00.170
adding another MeV
of energy to this.

00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:04.760
All right, this is one, and
the dominant energy production

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:05.870
mechanism within the sun.

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:07.790
But it's not the only one.

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:11.510
Also quite interesting is
the so-called carbon cycle.

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:15.360
It's contributing about 3%
to the sun's energy output.

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:20.630
So here, carbon basically
works as a catalyst.

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:25.670
So you have a carbon and, again,
a proton producing nitrogen.

00:04:25.670 --> 00:04:29.300
The nitrogen produces
carbon-13, carbon-13 together

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:33.770
with a proton nitrogen-14,
14, nitrogen-14 with a proton

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:39.230
oxygen-15, and oxygen-15,
nitrogen-15, and then,

00:04:39.230 --> 00:04:42.980
last but not least, nitrogen-15
together with a proton

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:48.950
produces carbon-12
again, and helium.

00:04:48.950 --> 00:04:54.450
So you see that the carbon-12
is the catalyst here,

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:57.840
which is used to produce
helium and energy.

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:01.940
So if you produce this cycle,
that you combine this cycle,

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:08.090
you find that, again, from four
protons or four hydrogen atoms,

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:12.530
you produce helium, positrons,
again, neutrinos, photons,

00:05:12.530 --> 00:05:15.410
and energy-- very
similar, it's visible,

00:05:15.410 --> 00:05:18.470
to this combined chain,
with the exception

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:19.970
that there is one
additional photon.

00:05:19.970 --> 00:05:24.200
And again, here, the positrons
supply additional energy

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:27.730
and they annihilate
with electrons.

00:05:27.730 --> 00:05:30.170
Great, so we have seen
that we do two things.

00:05:30.170 --> 00:05:34.840
We create heavier forms of
metal starting from hydrogen,

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:36.370
and we produce energy.

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:38.580
And so this is the energy
production mechanism

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:39.360
within the sun.

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:43.600
This is nice.

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:47.700
You produce-- you start
with hydrogen or deuterium.

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:51.300
Those are two elements
which are very abundant.

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:52.795
And you produce energy.

00:05:52.795 --> 00:05:54.420
So the question comes
up whether or not

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:57.300
you can actually
use this on Earth,

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:59.280
in a controlled
environment, in order

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:03.420
to produce energy and solve
many of the ongoing issues we

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:05.760
have on this planet.

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:07.360
There are several
efforts underway.

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:09.900
And this goes back all
the way to the '50s

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:12.580
of the last century.

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:16.890
The most prominent one currently
is the so-called ITER project,

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:19.350
which is international
collaboration,

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:23.940
and a project where one
tries to build a fusion

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:27.760
reactor in France.

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:31.390
I can already tell you that
the next stage for this

00:06:31.390 --> 00:06:36.580
is in about five years
to complete the project,

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:38.020
or complete the production of--

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:41.500
construction of the
project, and produce energy

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:43.480
for the first time
with this project

00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:44.800
in this controlled environment.

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:48.190
It will take another 10,
15 years on this road

00:06:48.190 --> 00:06:52.480
map of research in order to
produce, or be able to produce,

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:55.210
nuclear power reactors--

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:57.190
nuclear fusion
power reactors which

00:06:57.190 --> 00:06:59.920
can be used in some
sort of commercial way.

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:01.870
There's a few other
interesting projects

00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:04.510
which use different
magnet technologies which

00:07:04.510 --> 00:07:10.510
might have a more faster
pathway to success.

00:07:10.510 --> 00:07:13.210
But coming back
to the story here,

00:07:13.210 --> 00:07:17.920
so you could start
with protons again

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:20.390
in a proton-proton reaction.

00:07:20.390 --> 00:07:23.410
But it turns out that this
is a rather slow process,

00:07:23.410 --> 00:07:28.390
and not very promising
for a controlled reaction.

00:07:28.390 --> 00:07:32.320
But deuterium or tritium
are also very promising.

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:35.020
Note that, for
deuterium here, you

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:37.450
have to overcome the same
Coulomb barrier, right?

00:07:37.450 --> 00:07:40.600
The charges involved
are the very same.

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:42.250
But the cross
sections are higher.

00:07:42.250 --> 00:07:45.560
And therefore, the likelihood
for the process to occur

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:46.060
is higher.

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:49.270
And therefore, this
can happen much faster.

00:07:51.940 --> 00:07:54.940
Deuterium is-- again, as I was
saying before-- very abundant.

00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:56.830
You can just extract
it from water.

00:07:56.830 --> 00:07:59.980
Tritium is a little bit more
difficult to deal with--

00:07:59.980 --> 00:08:02.440
to produce and to
control because it's

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:07.920
radioactive and has some
really not so good features.

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:10.090
But this is a model
picture of ITER.

00:08:10.090 --> 00:08:13.490
Again, this is an
international project.

00:08:13.490 --> 00:08:17.310
It has a rather bad
reputation these days.

00:08:17.310 --> 00:08:20.685
It's by far the most
expensive scientific endeavor.

00:08:23.710 --> 00:08:26.330
But again, I mean, I
think this is investment

00:08:26.330 --> 00:08:29.250
to the future of this planet.

00:08:29.250 --> 00:08:31.680
And hopefully it will
succeed in the next years

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:33.360
with this project,
and in the long run

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:36.809
with having nuclear fission--

00:08:36.809 --> 00:08:38.820
fusion avail--
sorry, nuclear fusion

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:41.490
available for energy creation.

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:43.740
What you see here is--

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:50.460
the key feature of this
reactor is toroidal magnets

00:08:50.460 --> 00:08:53.370
which confine the plasma.

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:55.800
And also, electric
fields are used in order

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:57.360
to heat the plasma up first.

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:00.210
So you have to provide
heat to a point

00:09:00.210 --> 00:09:03.660
that the heat being produced
in the fusion process

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:06.630
is sufficient to self-sustain.

00:09:06.630 --> 00:09:12.990
And so confining the plasma
and providing enough energy,

00:09:12.990 --> 00:09:15.570
and the radiation in
this and so on, this

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:19.910
is all very difficult
problems to solve.

